          "text": "Verified two things that change the design surface. Both concrete.\n\n## Your github example doesn't work as written\n\n```json\n\"args\": [\"run\", \"-i\", \"--rm\", \"-e\", \"GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN\", \"ghcr.io/github/github-mcp-server\"]\n```\n\nBare `-e VAR` tells docker \"inherit `VAR` from *my* environment.\" But the transport doesn't pass llame's environment through — both `@ai-sdk/mcp`'s `getEnvironment` and the official SDK's `getDefaultEnvironment` build the child env from a fixed sudo-inspired allowlist (`HOME`, `LOGNAME`, `PATH`, `SHELL`, `TERM`, `USER`) plus whatever `env{}` you declare. `GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` is not on that list, so docker receives an unset variable and the server starts unauthenticated. Claude Code's own docs work around this by declaring it explicitly:\n\n```json\n\"env\": { \"GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN\": \"${input:github_token}\" }\n```\n\nThis is *good news* for llame — it forces the secret through the config file, which means `{env:GH_PAT}` resolution, which means the resolved value lands in `normalizeProtectedValues` exactly like a header value does today. The redaction invariant survives for free. The failure mode to guard is the reverse: an operator using the bare pass-through form gets a secret llame doesn't know about and therefore can't strip from stderr or errors. Worth a boot-time rejection or at minimum a docs callout.\n\n## The API process has to spawn too\n\nI wanted to scope spawning to `runs`-consuming processes. Can't: `chats.module.ts` imports `McpRuntimeModule` and `chat-loop.service.ts` calls `snapshotCandidates` to author the availability manifest at Run-accept. The API's catalog *is* a live connection — discovery has no offline form. So in a split topology every `web`-profile process holds every child process. Co-located default (`all`) is 1×M and fine, which is almost certainly your deployment; split is where it multiplies. Not a stdio flaw, an artifact of API-side snapshot authoring, but it's the thing to write down in `docs/scaling.md` rather than discover later.\n\n---\n\n## Where the HTTP lifecycle semantics don't transfer\n\nThis is the real design work, and it's larger than the transport. Today's contract (`docs/mcp-tools.md` \"Runtime behavior\"):\n\n| shipped HTTP behavior | under stdio |\n|---|---|\n| eager connect at boot | **eager spawn at boot** — chromium/docker running from process start, whether or not anyone chats. Cheap for a socket, not for a browser. |\n| reconnect with full jitter, `min(5min, 2^n)`, **forever** | a crash-looping server (missing binary, bad `npx`, image pull fail) respawns forever at ≤5min. Fine for a TCP connect; it's a process spawn + possible network fetch. Needs a terminal failure state or a much colder ceiling. |\n| complete catalog refresh every 48–72 min | for HTTP that's one `tools/list`. Does a stdio refresh mean re-listing on the live child, or recycling the process? Re-listing is right, but then a stdio child lives for the entire process lifetime — days. Memory/fd growth is now llame's problem. |\n| disconnect withdraws tools, retains remembered ids | maps cleanly. `onclose` fires on child exit. Good — this part transfers. |\n\nLibreChat shipped `startup: false` for exactly the first row: don't connect this one at boot. That's a reasonable field to steal, though \"lazy spawn on first advertisement\" is strictly better and doesn't need operator input.\n\nSecond-order: `npx -y @playwright/mcp@latest` does a **network fetch at every spawn**, in every process. `@latest` means the catalog can change between two restarts of the same config — which walks straight into llame's declaration-hash/drift machinery. It'll behave correctly (drift withdraws the tool), but the operator sees mysterious tool churn. Docs should push pinned versions, and boot on an air-gapped host will fail on the fetch, not on anything llame reports usefully.\n\n## stderr has a genuine tension\n\nDiscard it and \"my server won't start\" is undebuggable — stderr is where every stdio MCP server puts its diagnostics. Inherit it and you've written whatever the child printed into llame's logs, which is the one thing the protected-value architecture forbids.\n\nThe answer that satisfies both: `stderr: 'pipe'`, bounded ring buffer per server (last N KiB), run through `sanitizeProtectedValueJson` before emission, surfaced at a diagnostic log level with the server id. Same sanitizer, new input. The infrastructure exists.\n\n## Build shape: implement `MCPTransport`, don't wrap\n\nI looked at whether llame can wrap `Experimental_StdioMCPTransport`. It can't usefully — the `ReadBuffer` and the `spawn` call are both private, and those are precisely the two things needing changes. What llame needs:\n\n- **bounded read buffer** — both the AI SDK's and the official SDK's `ReadBuffer` do `Buffer.concat` with no cap (verified in both). A server that emits a newline-less stream grows llame's heap without limit. The HTTP path caps at 1 MiB; match it.\n- **kill ladder** — port the official SDK's `stdin.end()` → 2s → `SIGTERM` → 2s → `SIGKILL` (`dist/esm/client/stdio.js:155,164`). The AI SDK copy dropped this and only sends SIGTERM via the spawn `signal`.\n- **process group** — `detached: true` + `kill(-pid)`. `npx` → `npm` → `node` → chromium orphans otherwise, and reconnect-forever then stacks orphans on orphans.\n- **protocol pin** — the library accepts `2024-11-05`, llame's three revisions don't include it. Simplest hook: read `transport.protocolVersion` after connect (the client writes it there) and close if it's outside llame's set. Cleaner than the fetch interceptor the HTTP path needs.\n\nThat's ~200 lines implementing `MCPTransport` directly against `node:child_process`, with `createMCPClient({ transport: instance })` — confirmed supported, `isCustomMcpTransport` branches on it. Also sheds the `Experimental_` prefix on a pinned 1.0.x line.\n\n`shell: false` always. Never accept a single-string command; `command` + `args[]` is a security property, not just a format convention.\n\n---\n\n## Config surface, concretely\n\n```jsonc\n{\n  \"mcpServers\": {\n    \"github\": {\n      \"type\": \"stdio\",\n      \"command\": \"docker\",\n      \"args\": [\"run\", \"-i\", \"--rm\", \"ghcr.io/github/github-mcp-server\", \"--read-only\"],\n      \"env\": { \"GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN\": \"{env:GH_PAT}\" },\n      \"cwd\": \"/opt/llame\"            // optional\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n`type: \"stdio\"` explicit (matches the strict-closed schema style; Claude Code's own `add-json` emits it, so `.mcp.json` compatibility holds). `{env:…}`/`{path:…}` interpolation in `command`, `args`, and `env` values — same positions Claude Code expands `${VAR}` in, and `{path:…}` is a better fit than VS Code's `envFile` for docker/k8s secret mounts. Everything resolved goes into the protected-value set.\n\nSkip `envFile`, `dev`, `sandboxEnabled`, `inputs` — VS Code needs `inputs` because it prompts an interactive user; llame is a daemon with a resolver already.\n\n---\n\n## The one thing you haven't addressed\n\nYour stdio-only personal servers — what do they do? The current model is \"an allowlist entry is the operator's attestation that the tool is read-only,\" and write/send/delete/execute are prohibited outright. If those servers are read-only readers, nothing changes. If they're the kind of thing you'd actually want an assistant doing on your own box, then stdio is arriving *with* the policy question attached, and the honest sequencing is either:\n\n- **stdio first, read-only constraint intact** — smaller change, ships clean, and your write-capable servers stay unusable until #133; or\n- **pull #133 forward** and land stdio into a model that has capability grants, which is a much bigger slice.\n\nSilently letting stdio be the transport where the read-only rule gets relaxed because \"the admin set it up\" would be the bad outcome — that argument justifies *configuring* the server, not *advertising a write tool to a model driven by conversation content*. Those are different trust decisions and the config file only makes the first one.\n\nWhich is it for your servers?",
