            "content": "# stdio MCP research — config surface, transport gaps, peer practice\n\nNoncanonical research. Date: 2026-08-12. Scope: adding operator-configured\n**stdio** MCP servers to the instance-scoped `mcpServers` map, alongside the\nshipped Streamable HTTP transport. Transport, configuration surface, and\nprocess lifecycle only.\n\nThis document does **not** reopen the authorization contract. Tool id form\n(`mcp__<server>__<tool>`), the `tools.allowed` exact/wildcard filter, the\n\"operator attests read-only, remote annotations grant nothing\" model,\ndrift-withdraws-the-tool, receipt/snapshot exactness, and redaction scope were\nsettled for HTTP in\n[`2026-08-07-mcp-transport.md`](2026-08-07-mcp-transport.md) and the archived\n`add-streamable-http-mcp-tools` change. stdio changes the transport, the\nprocess lifecycle, and **where secrets live**; nothing above that line.\n\nThe read-only constraint stays an operator assumption with no new enforcement —\nthat was an explicit decision when this research was commissioned, not an\noversight. A capability/policy engine remains #133.\n\nMethod: read `@ai-sdk/mcp@1.0.67`'s shipped `src/` as installed in this repo's\nsibling checkout, read `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk@1.29.0`'s compiled stdio\nclient for comparison, read llame's own MCP module for the integration points,\nand surveyed four MCP-consuming products' documented stdio config surface. No\nchild process was spawned in the course of this research — see \"what I could\nnot verify.\"\n\n| source                              | pin                                              | how read                              | why                                                              |\n| ----------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `@ai-sdk/mcp`                       | `1.0.67` (the `ai-v6` line, pinned in `pnpm-workspace.yaml:39`) | installed `src/` at `~/Projects/llame` | llame's MCP client; the stdio transport it would use             |\n| `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk`         | `1.29.0` (transitive via `shadcn`, not a runtime dep) | installed `dist/esm/`                 | the reference stdio client both implementations descend from     |\n| llame                               | `adcd23f1`, branch `feature/mcp-stdio`           | working tree                          | integration points and existing HTTP hardening                   |\n| Claude Code MCP docs                | fetched 2026-08-12                               | `code.claude.com/docs/en/mcp`         | the `.mcp.json` format llame claims compatibility with           |\n| VS Code MCP configuration reference | fetched 2026-08-12                               | `code.visualstudio.com/docs/agents/reference/mcp-configuration` | the strictest documented stdio schema                            |\n| opencode                            | `0bff28de0` (2026-08-09), cached checkout        | `packages/sdk/js/src/v2/gen/types.gen.ts:1825-1840` | same stack; the one peer with a dissenting command shape         |\n| LibreChat MCP docs                  | fetched 2026-08-12                               | `librechat.ai/docs/features/mcp`      | closest multi-user self-hosted product that ships embedded stdio |\n| Open WebUI / `mcpo`                 | fetched 2026-08-12                               | docs + repo README                    | closest peer that **refused** embedded stdio                     |\n| github-mcp-server                   | `main` README, fetched 2026-08-12                | raw README                            | one of the two motivating examples                               |\n| playwright-mcp                      | `main` README, fetched 2026-08-12                | raw README                            | the other motivating example                                     |\n\n---\n\n## Finding 0 — both motivating examples have an HTTP path; the case for stdio rests on the long tail\n\nRecorded so the tradeoff is legible, not to relitigate it.\n\n- **playwright-mcp** documents a long-running service variant binding a port\n  (`--headless --browser chromium --no-sandbox`, port 8931 in the README's\n  example), which llame's shipped `streamable-http` entry consumes today.\n- **github-mcp-server** documents a GitHub-hosted remote at\n  `https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/` (PAT or OAuth), described in its README\n  as the easiest path.\n\nSo neither example is blocked by transport; both are blocked by *the operator\nhaving to run and supervise a second process or trust a hosted endpoint*. The\ndecisive argument for embedding is the **long tail**: MCP servers that ship\nstdio only and have no HTTP mode at all (the operator's own scripts, most\ncommunity filesystem/sqlite/notes servers). For those, the alternative is a\nper-server bridge (`mcpo`, `supergateway`), which is the same supervision cost\nmultiplied by server count.\n\nCorollary worth carrying into the proposal: **stdio does not unlock\nplaywright-mcp under the current policy.** Its catalog is `browser_click`,\n`browser_navigate`, `browser_type`, `browser_evaluate`,\n`browser_run_code_unsafe`, cookie/localStorage setters. Playwright annotates\neach tool with a `readOnly` boolean, but llame's spec says remote annotations\ngrant nothing. Under \"operator attests read-only,\" roughly the\n`browser_snapshot` / `browser_console_messages` half is allowlistable.\ngithub-mcp-server is the better exemplar because it ships an actual\n`--read-only` flag plus `--toolsets` / `GITHUB_TOOLSETS` scoping\n(`GITHUB_TOOLSETS` takes precedence over the flag, per its README).\n\n---\n\n## Finding 1 — the client already ships stdio, and accepting a custom transport instance is the only guard hook\n\n`@ai-sdk/mcp@1.0.67` exposes a second entry point, `./mcp-stdio`\n(`package.json` `exports`), which exports the transport under an experimental\nalias:\n\n```\nsrc/tool/mcp-stdio/index.ts:2   StdioMCPTransport as Experimental_StdioMCPTransport\n```\n\n`createMCPClient`'s option type is `transport: MCPTransportConfig | MCPTransport`\n(`src/tool/mcp-client.ts:230`) and the constructor branches on\n`isCustomMcpTransport(transportConfig)` (`:401-404`), assigning the instance\ndirectly when one is passed. **llame can supply its own `MCPTransport`\nimplementation.** This matters because every guard llame currently enforces on\nthe HTTP path lives inside a wrapped `fetch`\n(`apps/api/src/mcp/mcp-server-client.ts:612-708`, `protocolGuardedFetch` +\n`createMcpBoundedFetch`), and stdio has no `fetch` to wrap. The transport\ninterface is the only equivalent seam.\n\nConfidence: high — read from installed source.\n\n---\n\n## Finding 2 — the shipped stdio transport is minimal; every llame HTTP guard needs a stdio counterpart built\n\n`src/tool/mcp-stdio/mcp-stdio-transport.ts` is 157 lines.\n`create-child-process.ts` is 21. Point-by-point against what llame enforces\ntoday:\n\n| llame HTTP guard                                                                                  | stdio counterpart in `@ai-sdk/mcp@1.0.67`                                                                                                                                     |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| 1 MiB bounded request/response (`mcp-server-client.ts:31,612-624`, `mcp-bounded-fetch.ts`)        | **none.** `ReadBuffer.append` (`mcp-stdio-transport.ts:124-129`) does `Buffer.concat` with no cap; `readLine` (`:131`) only yields on `\\n`. A newline-less stdout stream grows llame's heap without bound. |\n| protocol pinned to `2025-03-26` / `2025-06-18` / `2025-11-25` (`mcp-server-client.ts:61-65,410-424`) | the library's own `SUPPORTED_PROTOCOL_VERSIONS` (`src/tool/types.ts:5-11`) additionally accepts **`2024-11-05`**, which llame excludes. Recoverable without interception: the client writes the negotiated version to `transport.protocolVersion` / `setProtocolVersion` (`mcp-client.ts:497-500`), so llame can gate on its own instance after connect. |\n| 5s close deadline then abort (`mcp-server-client.ts:36,1036-1053`)                                | `close()` is `this.abortController.abort()` and nothing else (`mcp-stdio-transport.ts:100-104`). The controller's signal is passed to `spawn` (`create-child-process.ts:13`), so Node kills the child with its default `killSignal` (`SIGTERM`) — **direct child only, no escalation, no process group.** |\n| session id treated as a protected value                                                           | not applicable; stdio has no sessions. Strictly simpler.                                                                                                                     |\n| secrets are header values, fed to `normalizeProtectedValues`                                      | secrets become **`env` values and `args`** (`--token=…`). Same machinery, different inputs. See Finding 3.                                                                    |\n| nothing reaches logs                                                                              | `stdio: ['pipe', 'pipe', config.stderr ?? 'inherit']` (`create-child-process.ts:11`). **Default `'inherit'` pipes the child's stderr into llame's process stderr** — a server that echoes a credential on a startup error writes it to the operator's logs. |\n\nBoth motivating examples spawn **process trees** (`npx` → `npm` → `node` →\nchromium; `docker run` → container runtime shim). SIGTERM to the direct child\nreaps none of the descendants, and llame's reconnect policy\n(`mcp-runtime.service.ts:24-25`, full jitter to a 5-minute cap, unbounded\nattempts) would respawn on top of them.\n\nConfidence: high for what the source does; **moderate** for the Node\n`signal`→`SIGTERM` claim, which is read from Node's documented `child_process`\nsemantics rather than exercised here.\n\n---\n\n## Finding 3 — child environment is a fixed allowlist, so `docker run -e VAR` pass-through silently fails (and that failure is load-bearing for redaction)\n\n`getEnvironment` (`src/tool/mcp-stdio/get-environment.ts:11-26`) builds the\nchild environment from a fixed, sudo-inspired allowlist plus whatever the\nconfig declares:\n\n```\nPOSIX:  HOME, LOGNAME, PATH, SHELL, TERM, USER\nwin32:  APPDATA, HOMEDRIVE, HOMEPATH, LOCALAPPDATA, PATH,\n        PROCESSOR_ARCHITECTURE, SYSTEMDRIVE, SYSTEMROOT, TEMP,\n        USERNAME, USERPROFILE\n```\n\nValues beginning with `()` are skipped (a shellshock-era function guard). The\nofficial SDK has the byte-identical list plus `PROGRAMFILES`\n(`@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/dist/esm/client/stdio.js:8-24`) — the two\nimplementations share ancestry.\n\n**Consequence for the canonical github-mcp-server invocation.** The widely\ncopied form is:\n\n```\ndocker run -i --rm -e GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN ghcr.io/github/github-mcp-server\n```\n\nBare `-e VAR` means \"inherit `VAR` from my own environment.\" Since\n`GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` is not on the allowlist, the docker CLI receives\nit unset and the server starts unauthenticated. github-mcp-server's own README\nconfig does not rely on the pass-through — it declares\n`\"env\": {\"GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN\": \"${input:github_token}\"}` alongside\nthe bare `-e` flag, so the variable exists in the child's environment for\ndocker to forward.\n\nThis accident is **favourable** for llame and should be made a rule rather than\na footnote. A declared `env` value goes through llame's `{env:…}`/`{path:…}`\nresolver, which means the resolved secret is known to the process and can be\nadded to `normalizeProtectedValues` exactly as a header value is today\n(`mcp-server-client.