dule.ts:3,34`) and `ChatLoopService` calls\n`this.mcpRuntime.snapshotCandidates()` (`chat-loop.service.ts:137`) to author\nthe availability manifest when a Run is accepted. Discovery has no offline\nform: the catalog *is* a live connection.\n\n```\n        HTTP today                          stdio, same topology\n\n  api-1 ─┐                          api-1 ── child ── (browser/container)\n  api-2 ─┼──▶ one shared endpoint   api-2 ── child ── (browser/container)\n  wkr-1 ─┤    scaled independently  wkr-1 ── child ── (browser/container)\n  wkr-2 ─┘                          wkr-2 ── child ── (browser/container)\n                                      ×  M configured servers\n```\n\nIn the default co-located topology (`workers.all`, one process) this is 1 × M\nand unremarkable. In a split topology it is (API processes + worker processes)\n× M child processes, including on `web`-profile processes that never execute a\ntool. This is a property of API-side snapshot authoring, not of stdio, but\nstdio is where it becomes expensive. It belongs in `docs/scaling.md`.\n\nConfidence: high — read from llame's own wiring.\n\n---\n\n## Finding 6 — three lifecycle semantics do not transfer from HTTP, and one concurrency property changes meaning\n\n`docs/mcp-tools.md` \"Runtime behavior\" and\n`apps/api/src/mcp/mcp-runtime.service.ts:22-26` define today's contract.\n\n**a. Eager connect at boot → eager *spawn* at boot.** For HTTP this is a socket.\nFor stdio it means a browser or container alive from process start regardless\nof chat traffic. Lazy spawn looks like the fix but interacts badly with\nFinding 5: the availability manifest is authored at Run-accept from the\nprocess-local catalog, so a not-yet-spawned server is `unavailable` and the\n*first* Run emits an availability reminder and refuses the tool, succeeding\nonly on the second. **Eager is the semantically correct default**; the escape\nhatch, if one is wanted, is an operator `enabled: false` switch (LibreChat's\n`startup: false` is the same idea) rather than laziness.\n\n**b. Reconnect-forever is wrong for a spawn.** `RECONNECT_BASE_MS` 1s doubling\nto a `RECONNECT_CAP_MS` 5-minute cap with no attempt ceiling is correct for a\ntransient network outage and pathological for a typo'd `command`, a missing\nimage, or an `npx` package that does not exist — those respawn forever. The\ndistinction worth encoding:\n\n```\nspawn fails (ENOENT) or the child exits within N seconds of spawn,\nK times consecutively        → terminal \"misconfigured\", stop until restart\nconnected + discovered, then the child dies\n                             → today's backoff, unbounded, unchanged\n```\n\nThe first is a configuration error and should be reported as one; the second is\nan outage and the existing semantics are right.\n\n**c. Catalog refresh should re-list, never recycle.** The 48–72 minute jittered\nrefresh is one `tools/list` on HTTP. Doing it by respawning the child would\nturn a routine refresh into a browser restart. Re-listing on the live child is\ncorrect, with the consequence that a stdio child lives for the whole process\nlifetime — days — so its memory and file-descriptor growth becomes llame's\noperational problem.\n\n**d. Concurrency means something different.** One child per server per process,\nshared by every concurrent Run in that process. JSON-RPC id multiplexing makes\nthis protocol-correct, and it is no different from HTTP (one client, one\nsession, shared). But for *stateful* stdio servers the sharing is visible:\n`workers.all.runs: 8` means eight Runs driving one browser, contending over\ntabs and navigation state. Filesystem, sqlite, and search servers do not care.\nBrowser servers do. This is a documentation warning; per-Run server instances\nwould be a substantially larger design and are not recommended.\n\n**e. `@latest` is a hidden network dependency.** `npx -y pkg@latest` fetches on\nevery spawn in every process: the catalog can change between two restarts of an\nunchanged config (surfacing as declaration drift, which llame handles correctly\nbut confusingly), and boot on an air-gapped host fails at the fetch rather than\nanywhere llame can explain. Docs should push pinned versions.\n\nConfidence: high for the mechanics; the crash-loop thresholds (N seconds, K\nattempts) are a proposal, not a finding.\n\n---\n\n## Finding 7 — the peer config surface has converged on `command` + `args[]` + `env{}`\n\nThe \"how do others do it\" question, from primary docs and generated types:\n\n| product                             | `type`                                    | command shape                            | env                                | secrets                                                                      | extras                                                                          |\n| ----------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Claude Code** (`.mcp.json`)       | `\"stdio\"`, inferred when `command` present | `command` + `args[]`                     | `env{}`                            | `${VAR}` and `${VAR:-default}`, expanded in `command`, `args`, `env`, `url`, `headers` | `timeout` (ms, per-tool-call); `MCP_TIMEOUT` env var for startup. `cwd` not documented on that page. |\n| **VS Code** (`mcp.json`)            | `\"stdio\"` **required**                    | `command` + `args[]`                     | `env{}` (string/number/null), `envFile` | `inputs[]` (`promptString` with `password: true`), referenced `${input:id}`  | `cwd`, `dev` (watch/debug), `sandboxEnabled` (macOS/Linux only)                 |\n| **opencode**                        | `\"local\"`                                 | `command: string[]` — **fused, no split** | `environment{}`                    | not surfaced in the config type                                              | `cwd`, `enabled`, `timeout`                                                     |\n| **LibreChat** (`librechat.yaml`)    | inferred                                  | `command` + `args[]`                     | `env{}`                            | `${ENV_VAR}`; `customUserVars` for per-user credentials                      | `startup: false`, `chatMenu`, `serverInstructions`; `initTimeout`/`timeout` documented on HTTP examples |\n\nConvergence is strong on **`command` + `args[]` + `env{}` (+ `cwd`)**.\nopencode's fused `command: string[]` is the only dissent and the minority.\n\nImplications for llame, given that README, `docs/mcp-tools.md`, and CHANGELOG\nall advertise the `mcpServers` map as `.mcp.json`-compatible:\n\n- Field names are effectively forced: `command`, `args`, `env`, `cwd`.\n- **`type: \"stdio\"` should be explicit**, not inferred. Inference requires\n  `oneOf` discrimination on property presence, which fights llame's\n  strict-closed, `required: [\"type\", …]`, `additionalProperties: false` schema\n  style — and Claude Code accepts explicit `\"stdio\"` too (its own\n  `claude mcp add-json` emits it), so portability is unharmed.\n- llame's existing `{env:…}` / `{path:…}` interpolation maps onto exactly the\n  positions Claude Code expands `${VAR}` in. `{path:…}` is a better fit than\n  VS Code's `envFile` for docker/k8s secret mounts.\n- `inputs[]`, `envFile`, `dev`, and `sandboxEnabled` should be skipped. VS Code\n  needs `inputs` because it prompts an interactive human; llame is a daemon\n  with a resolver.\n- `shell: false` is a security property, not a formatting convention: never\n  accept a single-string command line.\n\nConfidence: high for Claude Code, VS Code, and opencode (docs and generated\ntypes read directly). **Moderate** for LibreChat — the fetched page did not\nshow `env` / `timeout` / `initTimeout` on a stdio example specifically, so\nwhether those fields apply to stdio entries is unconfirmed.\n\n---\n\n## Finding 8 — the products architecturally closest to llame declined embedded stdio\n\nContext, deliberately overridden by this project's decision, but recorded\nbecause it names the failure modes to design against.\n\n- **Open WebUI** — self-hosted, multi-tenant, web-facing, i.e. llame's shape —\n  supports only Streamable HTTP natively and ships [`mcpo`](https://github.com/open-webui/mcpo),\n  a separate stdio→OpenAPI proxy. Its stated reason is that long-lived stdio\n  connections are hard to maintain securely across users and sessions in a\n  multi-tenant web app rather than a desktop process.\n- **LibreChat** embeds stdio and documents it against itself: it \"works well\n  for local, single-user environments\" and is \"not scalable for remote or cloud\n  deployments.\"\n- **Docker MCP Gateway** supports stdio but recommends streamable HTTP for\n  production, on the grounds that stdio \"works for single-machine setups but\n  breaks across container networks.\"\n\nThe self-hosted-personal-first deployment llame targets is exactly the case\nthese caveats do *not* condemn: one host, one operator, co-located topology,\nadmin-authored config. The caveats bite in the split topology (Finding 5) and\nin the supervision gaps (Findings 2 and 6), which is where the build effort\nshould go.\n\nDocker-in-docker / docker-from-docker socket exposure was raised and\nexplicitly ruled out of scope for this slice by the operator; it is noted here\nonly so a future reader knows the omission was deliberate rather than missed.\n\n---\n\n## Cross-source comparison\n\n| question                          | `@ai-sdk/mcp@1.0.67` stdio             | `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk@1.29.0`   | llame HTTP today                    | llame must BUILD for stdio                          |\n| --------------------------------- | -------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------ | ----------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |\n| custom transport accepted         | yes (`MCPTransportConfig \\| MCPTransport`) | n/a (different interface)            | uses config form + wrapped `fetch`  | own `MCPTransport` implementation                   |\n| read-buffer bound                 | **none**                               | **none**                             | 1 MiB request/response              | **yes — cap at the same 1 MiB**                     |\n| teardown                          | `abort()` → SIGTERM, direct child only | `stdin.end()`→SIGTERM→SIGKILL, 2s each | 5s deadline then abort              | **yes — port the ladder, add process group**        |\n| process-group / tree kill         | no                                     | no                                   | n/a                                 | **yes — `detached: true` + `kill(-pid)`**           |\n| stderr policy                     | `'inherit'` by default                 | `'inherit'` by default               | nothing reaches logs                | **yes — pipe, bound, sanitize, then log**           |\n| protocol pin                      | accepts `2024-11-05`                   | broader still                        | exactly three revisions             | **yes — gate on `transport.protocolVersion`**       |\n| child env                         | fixed allowlist + declared `env`       | same list + `PROGRAMFILES`           | n/a                                 | reject/flag bare `-e VAR` pass-through              |\n| secret redaction inputs           | n/a                                    | n/a                                  | header values + session id          | resolved `env` values + interpolated `args`         |\n| shell interpretation              | `shell: false`                         | `shell: false`                       | n/a                                 | keep; reject single-string command                  |\n| spawn-failure v