lly calls `McpServerClient.close()`, which happens to patch it over by calling `this.client.close()` → `this.onClose()` directly). The `'reports child exit through onDisconnect'` test doesn't catch this because it never has an in-flight call when the child exits, and it always calls `client.close()` afterward, which independently triggers the correct cleanup.\n\nFix direction: chain rather than replace, e.g. capture the existing `transport.onclose` before reassigning and call both, or wire the diagnostics/`notifyDisconnect` logic through the `onDisconnect` you already thread into `DefaultMCPClient` some other way (e.g. wrap `client.close`/rely on `onUncaughtError`/a dedicated transport subclass) instead of clobbering the property ai-sdk itself owns.\n\n**apps/api/src/mcp/mcp-stdio-transport.ts:39-42 (in `DiagnosticBuffer.append`) — a protected value straddling the `MAX_DIAGNOSTIC_CHARS` truncation point can leak unredacted**\n\n`append()` truncates the incoming chunk to fit the remaining `room`, then buffers/searches for newlines only in what was accepted. If a secret is split exactly at the truncation boundary, the truncated head (with a fragment of the secret) becomes part of `this.pending`. Once `retained &gt;= MAX_DIAGNOSTIC_CHARS`, every subsequent `append()` is a no-op (line 37 guard), so the terminator for that final partial line never arrives from `append`; it can only be released via `flush()` (called on close/protocol-mismatch), which calls `release()`/`redactProtectedString` on that single truncated line. Since the value being matched is now a partial substring of the actual protected value, `redactProtectedString`'s substring match won't find it, and the fragment (part of a credential) is emitted verbatim to `onDiagnostic`. This is a narrow, low-probability edge case (requires the secret to fall exactly across the 64 KiB cutoff), but it's a real gap in a security-sensitive redaction path, worth at least a comment/acknowledgment or a small safety margin (